# Fix for Recursive Default Argument Reference

The issue is that you're using `chat = chat` in the inner function's arguments, which creates a recursive reference. The inner `chat` tries to reference itself.

## Solution

Use a different parameter name in the inner function:

```r
generate <- function(chat = ellmer::chat_claude()) {

  function(input, chat_obj = chat) {
    ch <- chat_obj$clone()
    res <- ch$chat(input)
    list(result = res, chat = ch)
  }
}
```

## Alternative: Force evaluation

If you want to keep the same name, force evaluation of the outer `chat` first:

```r
generate <- function(chat = ellmer::chat_claude()) {
  force(chat)  # Force evaluation before creating inner function

  function(input, chat = chat) {
    ch <- chat$clone()
    res <- ch$chat(input)
    list(result = res, chat = ch)
  }
}
```

## Best Practice: Don't allow overriding

If users shouldn't override the chat object when calling the generated function, don't make it a parameter at all:

```r
generate <- function(chat = ellmer::chat_claude()) {

  function(input) {
    ch <- chat$clone()
    res <- ch$chat(input)
    list(result = res, chat = ch)
  }
}
```

This last approach is cleanest if the `chat` object should be fixed when `generate()` is called.

