The issue is that you have a parameter named `chat` that has a default value referencing itself (`chat = chat`). This creates a circular reference.

Here are two ways to fix it:

## Option 1: Rename the inner parameter

```r
generate <- function(chat = ellmer::chat_claude()) {

  function(input, chat_instance = chat) {
    ch <- chat_instance$clone()
    res <- ch$chat(input)
    list(result = res, chat = ch)
  }
}
```

## Option 2: Use a different default value

```r
generate <- function(chat = ellmer::chat_claude()) {

  function(input, chat = NULL) {
    if (is.null(chat)) {
      chat <- get("chat", parent.frame())
    }
    ch <- chat$clone()
    res <- ch$chat(input)
    list(result = res, chat = ch)
  }
}
```

## Option 3: Capture the outer chat explicitly (Recommended)

```r
generate <- function(chat = ellmer::chat_claude()) {
  default_chat <- chat

  function(input, chat = default_chat) {
    ch <- chat$clone()
    res <- ch$chat(input)
    list(result = res, chat = ch)
  }
}
```

**Option 3 is the cleanest** because it explicitly captures the outer `chat` value into a new variable (`default_chat`) that's used as the default for the inner function, avoiding any naming conflicts.

